		why: "PROMISE, the export's own docblock (:98): \"ACTIVE -> DEGRADED. §34.1's missing half: the assessment says VALIDATOR_FAILED, this says which validator.\" Declared as `statusChange('DEGRADED', 'ValidatorDegraded', ['ACTIVE'])` (:99) and ratified as an arrow at rph-domain/src/transitions.data.ts:2336-2341 (from ACTIVE, to DEGRADED, trigger `MarkValidatorDegraded`).\n\nWHO RELIES: §34.1's ratified error-handling rule, which requires that a validator implementation failure produce \"retry, ALTERNATE VALIDATOR IMPLEMENTATION, or escalation\" — an alternate can only be chosen if a failing implementation can be recorded as IMPAIRED without being WITHDRAWN. The file header (:22-27) makes that distinction the point of the command: §30's ASSESSING→VALIDATOR_FAILED records that an ASSESSMENT failed, this records that a VALIDATOR did, and RPH-ASR-006 keeps them apart. The victim pins the operational consequence of the distinction in Gate D: a DEGRADED validator's result still completes an assessment (:282-287) while a DISABLED one is refused (:268-275).\n\nWHAT THE MUTATION DOES: it retargets the arrow to DISABLED, collapsing \"impaired\" into \"withdrawn\" — every act of recording that a validator is failing would silently withdraw it, destroying the very alternate-selection §34.1 depends on and making the DEGRADED state unreachable again (the exact REG-E-024(c) defect this file was written to close). The mutant is single-limb by construction: ACTIVE→DISABLED is a LEGAL arrow on the machine, so `checkTransition` does not intervene, the command is still ACCEPTED, and nothing but an assertion naming DEGRADED can catch it. One did, at line 74.\n\nOBSERVED: Clean: `Tests 10 passed (10)`. Mutated: `Tests 4 failed | 6 passed (10)`. Failure [1/4] is validator-registry.test.ts:74 — `expect(statusOf()).toBe('DEGRADED')` → `AssertionError: expected 'DISABLED' to be 'DEGRADED'`, in `ACTIVE → DEGRADED → ACTIVE — degradation is recoverable`. The other three failures are consequential and in the same file (:89, the event-payload status at :131, and Gate D's DEGRADED case). Restored: sha256 back to 86ae62e5679245230ff0558b8963dd68b5a1bec823b83ed17bcdc54a6586db61, 6716 bytes, byte-identical.",
