		replace: "\t\ttarget: 'COMPOSABLE',",
		expectRed: ["packages/rph-application/src/handlers/recomposition.test.ts"],
		why: "The docblock names the regression this mutation restores, verbatim: \"This is the WIRE-3a wiring: before it, the handler unconditionally advanced to COMPOSABLE, so a recomposition with a detected conflict (or an unacceptable required child) was still marked composable — the §19-prohibited 'recomposition = concatenation' shortcut. Now a detected conflict forces CONFLICTED even when every child is individually SATISFIED, and an unacceptable required child or a failed whole-check forces INSUFFICIENT.\" Who relies on it: everything downstream of COMPOSABLE — it is the candidacy AcceptRecomposition settles, and through RecompositionContract.status SATISFIED it is what unblocks the parent's RECOMPOSING -> RECOMPOSED arrow. The mutation pins the target to 'COMPOSABLE' while leaving the evaluation, event selection and payload builders intact, so evaluateRecomposition still runs and still detects the conflict — its verdict is simply discarded. A parent whose children conflict, or whose required child was never assured, would be presented as composable and could be accepted and promoted on an integration that the engine itself had judged unsound, with the detected conflict recorded in the event stream but contradicted by the status.\n\nOBSERVED: Clean: 15 passed (15). Mutated: 5 failed | 10 passed (15). Killing assertion 'a detected conflict routes to CONFLICTED, not COMPOSABLE (recomposition is NOT concatenation)' at recomposition.test.ts:213 — expected 'COMPOSABLE' to be 'CONFLICTED'. Both INSUFFICIENT routings and the conflict-precedence test redden with it. CRUCIALLY the discriminating control 'an ACCEPTABLE child, no conflict, whole-checks hold -> COMPOSABLE' (:244-251) STAYS GREEN under the mutation — verified by name in a --reporter=verbose run — so the kill is about ROUTING, not about advancing at all. (The 5th failure is a differently-scoped control, 'CONTROL: the matching claim completes normally' at :313, which expects INSUFFICIENT and belongs to the parentCompletionClaimId guard group; the triage's prediction about the COMPOSABLE control at :251 was correct and I initially misread this one for it.) Restored by inverting the edit; byte-identical.",
