		why: "The file header states the promise: \"ProposeDecomposition creates a DecompositionContract already submitted for review (UNDER_REVIEW; the DRAFT→UNDER_REVIEW hop has no command).\" The birth declaration carries the ratified fact under REG-F-071: \"the machine declares `initialState: 'DRAFT'` and nothing writes it. Born UNDER_REVIEW.\" Who relies on it: ValidateDecomposition, whose precondition is `fromStates('UNDER_REVIEW')` and which is the only route to VALID / CONDITIONALLY_VALID / INVALID; and the birth census (verif/binding-row-truth.ts, arrow-command-census `birthStates`), which pins this as one of five rows where a machine's declared initialState LIES. The mutation makes the contract honour the machine's declared DRAFT instead — the plausible tidy-up a later agent would make from the one-line docblock. Both literals must move together because either alone is caught by kit.ts's `refuseOnBirthDrift`, a kill that would prove the drift checker works and say nothing about the birth status; moving both is the only way the victim's own claim is the thing that fails. Under it every decomposition is born in a state no command can advance, and the review gate the whole §14 decomposition pipeline hangs from becomes unreachable.\n\nOBSERVED: Clean: 10 passed (10) — re-verified immediately before the mutation after an earlier baseline read red on an UNRELATED test (see notes). Mutated: 2 failed | 8 passed (10). Killing assertion in 'drives a decomposition contract to VALID' — expected 'DRAFT' to be 'UNDER_REVIEW', i.e. lifecycle.test.ts:467 `expect(statusOf(DC)).toBe('UNDER_REVIEW')`, NOT a dispatch refusal, confirming the triage's check that DRAFT commits cleanly. The second failure is the downstream ValidateDecomposition test whose `fromStates('UNDER_REVIEW')` precondition no longer matches (expected 'REJECTED' to be 'ACCEPTED'). Restored by inverting the 9-line block; byte-identical.",
