		expectRed: ["packages/rph-application/src/handlers/decision-version-pin.test.ts"],
		why: "The mutated site carries the promise in the handler's own words: 'THE PIN IS NOW IMMUTABLE: it records the versions that existed when the decision was PROPOSED, which is what the approver reviewed.' The docblock adds the kind half -- ApproveDecision addresses any decision kind EXCEPT a waiver. WHO RELIES: RPH-GOV-003 and canon ASR-15 both rest on that version binding, and decisionAuthorizesVersions reads it at promotion to decide whether an approval has gone stale, so promoteBaseline and every professional relying on 'no green without assurance' depend on it. WHAT THE MUTATION DOES: it restores the write REG-F-017 deleted, letting the approver's stated number overwrite the proposal-time pin. A subject the engine never loaded gains a version nobody reviewed, and the staleness check at promotion then compares against a number the caller invented -- the engine performing the very laundering the rule forbids.\n\nOBSERVED: PASS=3 FAIL=0 -> PASS=2 FAIL=1, reproduced twice with identical mutant sha a3e38c4fdd8fca5c15c2934a5bd8a2140c24eb6d57fbb3b3fb1c8bdb85dee208. Failing test: 'REG-F-017: a Decision pins its subject versions at PROPOSAL > an accepted approval does not record a version for a subject the engine never saw' -- AssertionError 'the claim is not adopted: the pin still holds only what the store had': expected {...(2)} to deeply equal { int_01ARZ3NDEKTSV4RRFFQ69J7001: 1 }. Restored by inverting my own edit to baseline sha fdfe1c03b77e52d61024cf033b0f63fa1c7905a2603599231f6cacf3467f6a16; victim back to PASS=3 FAIL=0.",
