		why: "THE PROMISE, in the site's own header (kit.ts:212-222): \"THE CHAIN, NOT THE HOP — `commandId` already answers 'which command produced this event'... `causationId` answers a different question — what caused THE COMMAND — so an event inherits its command's cause rather than being handed its own command as its cause. THE NAIVE VERSION WAS WRITTEN INTO THE DESIGN RECORD AND IS WRONG... Causation is meaningful PRECISELY BECAUSE IT IS ABSENT on an act somebody actually issued.\" Ratified backing is PER-9 (JPWB-DOC-003 §9), cited at the victim file's head. WHO RELIES ON IT: every reader of the governed stream, and REG-E-030 in particular, which ruled that a cascade is attributed to the actor whose act triggered it — the victim's own header states the stake: \"Without causation, the governed record would say a human performed an act they did not.\" makeEvent builds EVERY event the engine commits, so this is the most-travelled export in the file. WHAT THE MUTATION DOES TO THEM: defaulting causationId to the dispatching command populates the field on all 446/446 events, duplicating `commandId` and erasing the only signal that distinguishes a derived act from one a person issued. If everything has a cause, nothing is marked as derived — the field survives and the CAUSATION does not. ⭑ This is the mutant the victim file NAMES in its own comment at :106-107, and it now exists rather than being described.\n\nOBSERVED: causation-provenance.test.ts 3/3 PASS -> 2 pass / 1 fail. Killing assertion :117 -> `AssertionError: causation marks a DERIVED act; populating it on an issued one duplicates \\`commandId\\` and marks nothing: expected [ …(468) ] to deeply equal []` — 468 issued acts acquired a fabricated cause. The other two tests stay GREEN: the opposite-direction test (a derived act names its true ORIGINATOR, not itself) and the correlation-axis test. That separation is the whole point of the `??` form; a flat `causationId: command.commandId` would have tripped both conjuncts and proved neither. Mutant compiles. Anchor: 1 hit, line 223, OWNERS [\"makeEvent\"] (span 189-226). ⚠ THIS VICTIM'S FIRST BASELINE WAS DISCARDED, NOT USED: it came back 0/3 with a seedWorkbench drive failure at #24 ValidateDecomposition, caused by another agent's live mutant in decomposition.ts — a file I do not own. Re-run in a window where no foreign production file was dirty, it was 3/3 green.",
